# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and built inside a Linux container,
# which is the exact situation where CRLF causes trouble that is hard to read
# from the error message.
#
# Two concrete failures this prevents:
#
#   1. A shell script checked out with CRLF fails inside the container with
#      "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory", or
#      silently misbehaves when a trailing ^M lands in a variable.
#
#   2. A patch generated on LF will not apply to a CRLF working copy, because
#      the context lines do not match byte for byte. That failure reports as
#      "patch does not apply" with no hint that line endings are the cause.
#
# Everything textual is normalized to LF in the repository. Git still checks
# out whatever the platform prefers for files not pinned below, but the ones
# that must be LF are pinned explicitly.

* text=auto

# Must be LF: executed by the container's shell.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Must be LF: gofmt emits LF, so anything else produces a permanent diff.
*.go            text eol=lf
go.mod          text eol=lf
go.sum          text eol=lf

# Generated and compared byte for byte by the differential harness.
*.tsx           text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf

# Patches carry their own line endings; rewriting them breaks application.
*.patch         -text
*.diff          -text

# PowerShell is happier with CRLF and never runs in the container.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf